@database "batch_monitor"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: batch_monitor.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Batch Monitor"
@{h1}Batch Monitor

Some models are run through a provider's batch API: cheaper, but an
answer can take minutes or hours. The server collects the requests of such
a model for a moment, sends them off as one job and waits for the provider
to finish it. This plugin shows what that machinery is doing: the requests
waiting to be sent, the jobs running at the providers, the ones that just
finished, and the totals since the server started. It is for watching
only -- it gives agents no tools and changes nothing.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Batch Queues panel with running and waiting jobs"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Batch Queues panel  " link panel}

For developers
  @{"  The two calls behind it  " link calls}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Batch Queues panel"
@{h1}The Batch Queues panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}System@{ub}, "Batch Queues") or the command
palette. It belongs to no session: it shows the batch work of the server,
whichever chat is open.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Batch Queues panel"}

@{h2}The figures

@{table}
Figure | Counts
Queues | the queues in the table below
Jobs running | the jobs listed that are not finished -- also one put together and not yet sent, and one being cancelled
Jobs completed | jobs the provider finished since the server started; below it, how many failed
Requests completed | requests answered in completed jobs; below it, how many failed -- in a failed job, every request counts as failed
Processing time | the mean time from sending a job to its end, with the shortest and the longest below; a dash before the first job has ended
@{body}

The totals are kept in memory and start at zero with each server start.
Jobs that expired or were cancelled are in neither count, but their time
is in the processing time.

@{h2}The table

A queue is one model at one batch provider, named
@{tt}provider:model@{utt}, for example
@{tt}openai_httpx:gpt-5.4@{utt}. Each job of a queue is a row -- the
running ones and those finished within the last three minutes -- and a
queue with requests waiting for its next job gets one more row with the
status @{i}pending@{ui}.

@{table}
Column | A job | The waiting requests
Job | its id; the tooltip is the provider's id for it | --
Status | pending (put together, not sent yet), submitted, validating, in progress, finalizing, cancelling, completed, failed, expired, cancelled | pending
Progress | requests answered of all in the job, and how many failed | how many are waiting
Input tokens | the estimate for all its requests, kept when the job finishes | the estimate for the waiting requests
Time | since it was sent; for a finished job, how long it ran | the collection window, "~10s"
@{body}

A job the server found at a provider when it started -- sent before a
restart, for a provider with @{tt}cancel_on_startup: false@{utt}; by
default the server cancels the jobs it had sent instead -- has no requests of its own: its progress, tokens and time are
dashes, and its id starts with @{tt}rec_@{utt}. A job that failed while
being sent shows "0 / 5" and the failed status: its requests never reached
the provider. A job sent again after the provider cancelled it or let it
expire counts its time from the new start.

The requests of a queue are sent when the collection window closes, or at
once when there are as many as a job may hold. The window is the first
provider's @{tt}collection_window_seconds@{utt} in @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt}; it
applies to all queues.

A click on a column head sorts the rows by it, a second click turns the
order; the table starts sorted by queue. The order you pick holds through
every reload and is kept in the browser for next time.

@{h2}Reloading

The panel loads anew every 5 seconds from the start, and not while it is
hidden. The toolbar reloads at once, turns the automatic reload off and on,
and the menu next to it sets another interval; the choice is kept in the
browser. Next to it, the time of the last update.

@{h2}When there is nothing to show

@{bullet}@{b}Batch processing is off@{ub}: the server runs no batch
manager -- no model uses a batch provider. See
@{" Setting it up " link setup}.
@{bullet}@{b}No batch jobs@{ub}: nothing is waiting, running or finished
in the last three minutes.
@{bullet}@{b}Batch queues could not be loaded@{ub}, with the server's
reason, when a call fails.

None of them keeps the figures or rows shown before, so nothing old passes
for current.
@endnode

@node calls "The two calls behind it"
@{h1}The two calls behind it

@{table}
Call | Answers
GET /plugins/batch_monitor/queues | queues and collection_window_seconds
GET /plugins/batch_monitor/metrics | the totals since the server started
@{body}

Both answer 503 when the server runs no batch manager.

@{h2}queues

@{tt}queues@{utt} lists the queues sorted by key; a queue with neither
waiting requests nor listed jobs is left out. Each has
@{tt}queue_key@{utt}, @{tt}pending_requests@{utt},
@{tt}pending_estimated_tokens@{utt} and @{tt}jobs@{utt} -- the running
jobs first, then those finished within the last 180 seconds. A job has
@{tt}job_id@{utt}, @{tt}provider_job_id@{utt} (null until it is sent),
@{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}total_requests@{utt},
@{tt}completed_count@{utt}, @{tt}failed_count@{utt},
@{tt}elapsed_seconds@{utt} (whole seconds; null for a job not sent) and
@{tt}estimated_input_tokens@{utt}.

@{code json}
{"queues": [{"queue_key": "openai_httpx:gpt-5.4", "pending_requests": 2, "pending_estimated_tokens": 240,
             "jobs": [{"job_id": "a1b2c3", "provider_job_id": "batch_abc", "status": "in_progress",
                       "total_requests": 5, "completed_count": 3, "failed_count": 1,
                       "elapsed_seconds": 125, "estimated_input_tokens": 610}]}],
 "collection_window_seconds": 10.0}
@{body}

@{h2}metrics

@{code json}
{"completed_jobs": 3, "failed_jobs": 1, "total_completed_requests": 1200, "total_failed_requests": 7,
 "processing_time": {"min": 0.0, "max": 3599.7, "mean": 1229.9}}
@{body}

The times are seconds to one decimal, null before the first job has
ended.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    batch_monitor:
      type: batch_monitor
      enabled: true
      description: "Monitor and query batch queue status"
@{body}

It has no settings and keeps no data; the panel is there as soon as the
plugin is on. What it shows comes from the server's batch manager, and
that exists only when @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt} sets batch processing up:
a @{tt}batch:@{utt} block with at least one enabled provider, and at least
one model with @{tt}provider: batch@{utt}. Without all three the panel
says batch processing is off.

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  batch:
    storage_path: "data/batch_jobs"
    providers:
      openai_httpx:
        enabled: true
        collection_window_seconds: 10
        max_requests_per_batch: 100
  models:
    gpt-sol-batch:
      provider: batch
      batch_provider: openai_httpx
      model: gpt-6-sol
      api_key: ${OPENAI_API_KEY}
@{body}

An agent sends its requests through the batch manager when it runs on
such a model; the queue key is the @{tt}batch_provider@{utt} and the
@{tt}model@{utt} of that entry.

@{h2}What it cannot see

@{bullet}Each process runs its own batch manager. The panel shows the one
of the API server: the requests of agents running in it. An agent started
from the command line, or a session woken in a process of its own, sends
its batches past the panel.
@{bullet}Finished jobs stay listed for three minutes; older ones and
every total are gone with a server restart.
@endnode
